# .cursorrules

Guidance for Cursor's AI (and future me) when working in this repo.

## Project
Continuum — an AI-powered work-memory tool. Users save a quick free-text
note before stopping work; AI structures it into Completed / Next Steps /
Open Questions / Context Summary. When they return, Continuum generates a
short "welcome back" briefing so they can resume without reconstructing
their train of thought. Capstone project for the FlyRank Frontend AI
Engineering track.

## Stack
- Next.js (App Router), JavaScript/TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS for styling
- Supabase for auth + Postgres database, with row-level security enforced
  at the database level (users only ever query their own rows)
- LLM API (Gemini free tier by default) — called only from server-side API
  routes, never from client components. API key must never reach the browser.
- Deployment target: Vercel

## Data model (initial)
- `projects` — id, user_id, name, created_at
- `context_snapshots` — id, project_id, raw_note, structured_output (JSON:
  completed/next_steps/open_questions/summary), created_at

## Conventions
- **Commits:** Conventional Commits format (`feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`,
  `docs:`, `refactor:`, `test:`) — one logical change per commit.
- **Components:** functional components only. Keep them small and
  accessible — semantic HTML first, ARIA only when semantic HTML can't do
  the job. Every interactive element must be keyboard-reachable.
- **API routes:** all AI calls go through `/app/api/*` routes. No API keys
  or secrets in client-side code, ever.
- **Cost control:** AI should be called at most twice per work session
  (save context, generate resume briefing) — never a continuous chat loop.
  Rate-limit AI calls per user.
- **Errors:** every AI call must handle loading, error, and empty states
  explicitly — if the AI call fails, the user's raw note must still be
  saved unstructured rather than lost. No silent failures.
- **Testing:** components should be written in a testable way from the
  start (Jest or Vitest) — target ≥50% coverage by the capstone deadline,
  not retrofitted at the end.

## What I want from Cursor's AI in this repo
- Point out accessibility issues (missing labels, poor contrast, no
  keyboard support) even if I didn't ask.
- Flag if a change would leak a secret to the client or skip row-level
  security on a new table/query.
- Flag if a change would add an unnecessary AI call (cost/burden risk).
- Prefer explaining a fix over silently rewriting large chunks of code —
  I need to be able to defend every line of this project.
